-- title:
--   Pigeonhole bound by the favoured cone fibre
-- statement:
--   A **double cone** in $\mathbb{R}^d$ is a pair $V = (a, \varphi)$ consisting of a unit axis $a \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and an apex angle $\varphi \in (0,\pi/2]$, with underlying set
--   $$V \;=\; \{\, h \neq 0 : \cos\varphi\,\|h\| < \langle a,h\rangle \,\} \;\cup\; -\{\, h \neq 0 : \cos\varphi\,\|h\| < \langle a,h\rangle \,\}$$
--   (Lean: `DCone`, with `V.carrier`). A **configuration** $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ is a map assigning such a double cone $\Gamma(x)$ to each point $x$ (`Configuration`). Let $Q \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a **finite** set of points and let $V$ be a double cone. Write
--   $$F(\Gamma, Q, V) \;=\; \{\, x \in Q \;:\; \Gamma(x) = V \text{ as subsets of } \mathbb{R}^d \,\} \qquad (\texttt{blockFibre } \Gamma\ Q\ V)$$
--   for the fibre of $\Gamma$ over $V$ inside $Q$ — the points of $Q$ whose cone has the same underlying set as $V$; types are compared as sets, not as axis–angle pairs. Assume $V$ is **favoured by majority in $Q$** (`FavoredIn Γ Q V`), i.e.
--   $$\#\,F(\Gamma,Q,W) \;\le\; \#\,F(\Gamma,Q,V) \qquad \text{for every double cone } W,$$
--   so that its fibre has maximal size. Assume finally that $\Gamma$ takes at most $L$ distinct values on $Q$, i.e. the image $\Gamma(Q)$ satisfies $\#\,\Gamma(Q) \le L$ for some $L \in \mathbb{N}$. Then
--   $$\#\,Q \;\le\; L \cdot \#\,F(\Gamma, Q, V).$$
--
--   This is the pigeonhole step of a majority argument: if the points of $Q$ are sorted into at most $L$ classes according to the cone the configuration gives them, then the largest class — the favoured one — contains at least a $1/L$ fraction of $Q$. It is what converts a finiteness statement about the number of cone types (a reduction to finitely many reference cones) into a *quantitative* lower bound on the size of a single homogeneous sub-collection, on which a common cone direction is then available.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Finiteness of $Q$ is an explicit hypothesis, needed because `Set.ncard` returns $0$ for an infinite set, which would make the inequality vacuous rather than false. `FavoredIn` itself is stated with `Set.encard` (cardinality in $\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$), so it is meaningful without the finiteness assumption. The bound $L$ is a natural number and need not be optimal; $L = 0$ forces $Q$ to be empty.
